if randomise is false, then the index of the item in the array should be item.position-1
whereas if randomize is true, the item can be placed anywhere in the resultant array

response:
 Based on your instructions and the given input array, here's how you can create a new array:

First, let's separate the items with `randomize` set to `false` from those with `randomize` set to `true`.

```javascript
const fixedItems = [];
const randomItems = [];

for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
  const item = arr[i];
  if (!item.randomize) {
    fixedItems.push(item);
  } else {
    randomItems.push(item);
  }
}
```

Next, we will place the fixed items in their correct positions based on their `position` property. We'll use an empty array `result` to store the final order of items.

```javascript
const result = [];

for (let i = 0; i < fixedItems.length; i++) {
  const fixedItem = fixedItems[i];
  result.unshift(fixedItem); // unshift adds the element at the beginning of the array
  result = result.slice(fixedItem.position); // slice removes the first 'fixedItem.position' elements from the array
}
```

Now, we will randomly place the items with `randomize` set to `true`. Since we don't know the exact number of random items, we cannot predict the final size of the result array. Therefore, we'll use a while loop to add random items until all items have been added.

```javascript
while (randomItems.length > 0) {
  const randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * result.length);
  const randomItem = randomItems.shift();
  result.splice(randomIndex, 0, randomItem);
}
```

