Regarding your Java code, it looks correct for calculating the duration between two dates in different time zones. However, I would recommend using `ZoneOffset` instead of `ZoneId` if you know the offsets are constant (e.g., UTC+8 or UTC-5), as it may be more efficient and simpler in certain use cases. Here's an example:
```java
import java.time.LocalDateTime;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.time.ZoneOffset;
public class DateDurationCalculator {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalDateTime start = LocalDateTime.of(2022, 1, 1, 10, 0); // London time
        LocalDateTime end = LocalDateTime.of(2022, 1, 1, 15, 30); // New York time
        ZoneOffset londonTimeZone = ZoneOffset.ofHours(0);
        ZoneOffset newYorkTimeZone = ZoneOffset.ofHours(-5);
        ZonedDateTime startZonedDateTime = ZonedDateTime.of(LocalDateTime.now(), londonTimeZone);
        ZonedDateTime endZonedDateTime = ZonedDateTime.of(LocalDateTime.now().plusHours(5), newYorkTimeZone); // assuming current system time in London
        Duration duration = Duration.between(startZonedDateTime, endZonedDateTime);
        System.out.println("Duration: " + duration);
    }
}
```
This version uses `LocalDateTime` instead of `ZonedDateTime` for the date and time components, and `ZoneOffset` instead of `ZoneId`. It also assumes the current system time in London and adds 5 hours to get the New York time. This approach may be simpler if you

